📅 Day 3 – Local LLM + FastAPI Integration (Ollama)
🚀 Overview

Today, I focused on learning how to set up a local Large Language Model (LLM) and integrate it with a backend API using FastAPI.

The primary objective was to:

Run an LLM locally on my system
Expose it through an API endpoint
Enable interaction between a user and the model via HTTP requests

This setup is essential for building AI-powered applications that are private, fast, and independent of external APIs.

🧠 What I Learned
1. Understanding Ollama

Ollama is a local runtime environment designed to run large language models directly on your machine.

Key takeaways:

It allows running models without relying on cloud services
Reduces latency and improves response speed
Enhances data privacy, since everything runs locally
Ideal for development, experimentation, and offline AI applications
2. Running Ollama Using Docker

To simplify installation and environment setup, I used Docker to run Ollama.

Steps involved:

Pulled the Ollama Docker image
Started a container with the required configuration
Exposed port 11434 to allow API access

Once running, Ollama provides a local API endpoint:

http://localhost:11434

This endpoint acts as a gateway to interact with the model programmatically.

3. Working with Models (Gemma2)

After setting up Ollama, I explored available models and chose to use the Gemma2 model.

What I did:

