This step helped me understand how model interaction works at a low level.

4. Building the FastAPI Backend

Next, I created a backend service using FastAPI, which is a modern Python framework for building APIs.

Key concepts learned:

Initializing a FastAPI application
Creating API endpoints (routes)
Handling incoming request data using request bodies
Structuring responses in JSON format
⚙️ Implementation
📄 Code Explanation
from fastapi import FastAPI, Body
from ollama import Client

app = FastAPI()

client = Client(host="http://localhost:11434")

@app.post("/chat")
def chat(
    message: str = Body(..., description="The Message")
):
    response = client.chat(
        model="gemma2",
        messages=[
            {"role": "user", "content": message}
        ]
    )

    return {"response": response["message"]["content"]}
🔍 What This Code Does
Initializes a FastAPI application
Connects to the local Ollama server
Creates a /chat endpoint
Accepts a user message in the request body
Sends that message to the LLM (gemma2)
Returns the generated response back to the user
🔁 System Flow Explanation
User → FastAPI → Ollama (LLM) → Response → User

Step-by-step flow:

The user sends a request to the /chat endpoint
FastAPI receives and processes the request
The message is forwarded to the Ollama client
The LLM (Gemma2) generates a response
FastAPI sends the response back to the user

This creates a complete AI interaction pipeline.

